Videos plugin for Geeklog 2.1.1
================================

Development version: 0.17.0

Compatibility
-------------

* Geeklog 2.1.1
* PHP 5.6 or later, using PHP 5.6-compatible syntax
* No plugin-owned database table
* YouTube Data API v3

Current state
-------------

The plugin currently provides automated Geeklog installation and upgrades,
permissions, native configuration, protected JSON storage, YouTube Data API
v3 searches, quota and cache management, a public catalogue and player,
pseudonymous qualified-view tracking, local ratings, registered-user history
and a first catalogue ranking and rotation algorithm.

Registered account retention
----------------------------

Video views, ratings and personal recommendations linked to a registered
Geeklog account are retained without an automatic expiration date. They are
deleted only when the user explicitly requests deletion or when the Geeklog
account itself is explicitly deleted.

The plugin stores a keyed pseudonymous account hash, never the Geeklog user ID,
name or email address in its JSON data files.

Database policy
---------------

The plugin creates no database table. Geeklog's existing core tables are still
used by Geeklog itself to register the plugin, permissions, groups and native
configuration values.

Catalogue ranking
-----------------

Since version 0.4.0, the catalogue reorders YouTube results already present in
the JSON cache without making an additional API request. Its score combines:

* original YouTube relevance;
* a Bayesian local rating;
* confidence based on the number of ratings;
* a bonus for videos not watched by the signed-in account;
* a bonus for priority channels;
* a small deterministic rotation.

The Bayesian rating uses a prior average of 3.5 out of 5 with a weight of five
ratings. A video with only one rating therefore cannot immediately dominate
the catalogue. Rotation changes every six hours and stays stable within the
same period. The max_same_channel setting is enforced on the ranked list.

Global video ranking
--------------------

Qualified views update one bounded aggregate JSON file per video. The global
ranking stores at most 500 entries and is updated incrementally after a view or
rating. Its score is:

    bayesian rating * 12
    + min(12, log(rating count + 1) * 4)
    + min(20, log(view count + 1) * 4)
    + watch ratio observed when the view qualifies * 15
    + activity recency (maximum 10, half-life 30 days)
    + publication recency (maximum 5, half-life 365 days)

The administration can rebuild this ranking from at most 5,000 existing video
statistic files. A failed aggregate or ranking update never invalidates the
qualified view or rating that was already stored.

Since version 0.5.3, a single validated playback-end event refines this ratio
to the completed progress without adding a second view.

Next-video recommendations
--------------------------

Version 0.6.0 selects next-video candidates from the originating search cache.
Only the opaque SHA-256 cache key is carried in internal links. If that cache
is unavailable, the global local ranking is used. The current video is always
excluded, account history is considered, and the configured channel-diversity
limit remains active. Suggestions never cause an additional YouTube API call
and never enable autoplay by themselves.

Since version 0.6.2, watched videos are removed before ranking and channel
diversity are applied. Registered-account history is excluded without a time
limit. For an anonymous visitor with tracking enabled and an existing plugin
cookie, views from the current and previous month are excluded. The plugin
returns fewer suggestions rather than filling the list with watched videos.

Recommendation signals
----------------------

Version 0.6.3 records only aggregated accepted and skipped counters for each
suggested video. Signals require a valid playback token and are limited to 60
events per pseudonymous visitor per hour. They contain the source and target
video IDs but no clear IP address. The ranking contribution is bounded:

    min(10, log(accepted + 1) * 3)
    - min(10, log(skipped + 1) * 2)

Navigation proceeds after the response or after 800 milliseconds, so a failed
statistics write cannot trap the visitor on the current page.

YouTube player modes
--------------------

Version 0.6.4 provides two compliant player modes. Standard uses controls=1
and fs=1. Minimal uses controls=0 and fs=0, which removes the control bar and
fullscreen button but also removes direct access to progress, volume and
captions. Both modes use rel=0, playsinline=1, iv_load_policy=3,
enablejsapi=1 and a validated origin. The plugin does not overlay, obscure or
block YouTube branding, links or final recommendations. YouTube retains
control of those elements.

Local channel ranking
---------------------

Version 0.7.0 derives a bounded local channel ranking from the global video
ranking without an additional YouTube API request. It aggregates at most the
500 retained video entries into at most 250 channels. Its score is:

    Bayesian channel rating with a 3.5/5 prior of 10 ratings * 12
    + rating confidence, capped at 12
    + view volume, capped at 18
    + average qualified watch ratio * 15
    + diversity of distinct videos rated at least 4/5, capped at 12
    + number of highly ranked videos, capped at 10
    + activity recency, capped at 10 with a 30-day half-life

The ranking is rebuilt incrementally whenever the global video ranking changes
and can also be reconstructed from the administration page. Because no SQL
table is used, channel statistics only cover videos retained in the bounded
global ranking; they are local indicators rather than exhaustive analytics.

Administration layout
---------------------

Version 0.7.1 presents administration information in responsive, theme-neutral
sections. Status indicators use a flexible grid, forms retain usable labels and
touch targets, and wide maintenance or ranking tables scroll horizontally on
small screens. This release changes presentation only.

Public rankings
---------------

Version 0.8.0 adds a read-only rankings.php page for every visitor. It reads
the bounded local JSON rankings and cached thumbnails without making a YouTube
API call. Administrators can disable the complete page, the video list, or the
channel list independently and can limit each public list from 1 to 50 items.
Blocked video and channel IDs are filtered again when the page is rendered.

Since version 0.8.1, the public page displays videos and channels in separate
accessible tabs. Only the selected ranking and its cached thumbnails are read.
Disabled ranking types are omitted, and the remaining type becomes the default.

Account data controls
---------------------

Version 0.9.0 completes the registered-user privacy controls on history.php.
When allowed by configuration, a signed-in user can download a CSRF-protected
JSON export containing profile preferences, ratings, recommendations and all
monthly view records. The export contains no Geeklog user ID, name, email
address, IP address, HMAC secret or YouTube API key.

Explicit deletion requires a checkbox, a browser confirmation and a valid CSRF
token. It removes the pseudonymous account files and the user's individual
rating entries. Rating aggregates and video/channel rankings are then rebuilt.
Non-identifying aggregate view totals cannot be attributed back to one account
and are retained. Without this explicit action, registered-account data remains
stored without automatic expiration.

Geeklog block
-------------

Version 0.10.0 implements the optional dynamic Geeklog block. It can display
recommended, top-rated, most-watched, recently active videos, or the best local
channels. The block reads only bounded JSON rankings and cached thumbnails and
never triggers a YouTube API call. Blocked IDs and the configured maximum number
of videos from one channel are enforced. The item count is configurable from
1 to 10 and defaults to 3.

Version 0.10.1 displays each block title below its thumbnail and limits the
visible label to six words while retaining the complete title as a tooltip.
The catalogue link is centered. During upgrades, the existing block_mode
configuration row is safely converted from a text field to Geeklog selection
array 3 without deleting the row or changing its saved value.

Version 0.10.2 gives every block mode a distinct title and displays the signal
that explains the ordering. Recommended uses the combined local score; rated
uses the Bayesian weighted rating; watched uses qualified local views; recent
uses the latest local viewing date; and channels use the local channel score.
The configuration labels now state these semantics explicitly.

Version 0.10.3 uses the configured public_title as the outer title of every
Geeklog block mode. A short inner label identifies the selected information
type. Per-item statistics are no longer rendered, while the sorting algorithms
remain unchanged. The best-channel mode uses a compact ranked list.

Version 0.10.4 adds a random video block mode. It shuffles only the bounded
video entries already present in the local JSON ranking, then applies blocked
video and channel filters and the configured per-channel diversity limit.
It performs no YouTube API request and no JSON write.

Version 0.10.5 adds a random-mode option. On every dynamic block generation it
uniformly chooses one of the six existing modes: recommended, weighted rating,
qualified views, recent local activity, random videos, or best channels. The
inner block label identifies the mode that was actually selected. Each selected
mode keeps its normal filtering and ranking behavior.

Video availability
------------------

Version 0.11.0 activates the JSON availability cache. Every videos.list detail
response already made after a search records whether each requested video is
public and embeddable. Requested IDs omitted by the response are marked as
not_found_or_private. No availability request is made while rendering a page.

Known unavailable videos are removed from cached searches, next-video
suggestions, public rankings, the Geeklog block and the watch page. A later
valid API response restores availability. The check lifetime defaults to 24
hours; unavailable status remains protective beyond expiration until refreshed.

Moderation
----------

Version 0.12.0 adds a CSRF-protected moderation page requiring the existing
videos.moderate permission. Decisions are stored as one bounded JSON document
per video or channel under moderation/videos and moderation/channels. Returning
an item to neutral deletes its decision file.

Videos can be blocked. Channels can be allowed, prioritized, blocked, disabled
or returned to neutral. Blocked and disabled content is immediately excluded
from fresh or cached searches, playback, next-video suggestions, public
rankings and the Geeklog block. Priority channels are merged with the legacy
configuration preference. Existing configuration block lists remain enforced
and therefore take precedence over an "allowed" moderation record.

Version 0.12.1 adds quick "Block video" and "Block channel" actions to the
public watch page for administrators holding the videos.moderate right. Both
actions use Geeklog CSRF protection, require confirmation, write to the same
segmented moderation JSON storage and return the moderator to the catalogue.

Version 0.12.2 isolates quick moderation on a dedicated confirmation page.
Geeklog 2.1.1 security tokens are single-use, so playback AJAX events can no
longer consume the token used by a later moderation decision.

Version 0.13.0 makes manual channel management understandable without finding
a technical YouTube identifier first. The moderation page lists up to 500
channels already known from cached channels.list responses, sorted by visible
channel name. The UC identifier remains visible and an advanced manual entry
is retained as a fallback. Building this list never triggers a YouTube API call.

Version 0.14.0 adds an optional short-form exclusion policy. Because YouTube
Data API v3 has no reliable Shorts flag, the policy conservatively excludes
every video at or below a configurable duration (180 seconds by default).
It is disabled by default and is enforced for searches, cached catalogues,
recommendations, public video rankings and the Geeklog video block.

Version 0.14.1 adds a recommended "probable Shorts" mode. It excludes a video
under the configured duration only when its title, description or tags contain
an explicit Shorts marker. The previous duration-only behavior remains
available as strict mode.

Version 0.15.0 adds a bounded permanent catalogue pool stored in
rankings/permanent_pool.json. By default it retains up to 24 videos, reserves
up to 25 percent of the catalogue, and automatically admits videos with at
least three ratings and a Bayesian weighted rating of 4.0/5. Administrators
can pin, remove, exclude, readmit and rebuild entries. Availability,
moderation, blocked lists, Shorts filtering and channel diversity always take
precedence. A successful new rating marks the pool for lazy reconstruction.

Version 0.15.1 adds a bounded compatible-cache fallback. When the exact search
cache is unavailable and YouTube or the local quota prevents a refresh, the
plugin inspects at most 500 cached searches and selects the newest unexpired
stale entry with the same query, order, SafeSearch, language, region,
publication date, category and remote channel filter. Current local duration,
Shorts, allow/block and moderation rules are reapplied. The filtered result is
stored under the current key for five minutes, without another API call.

Version 0.16.0 adds server-side SEO metadata for public pages without exposing
the YouTube API key or making additional API calls. Valid watch pages receive
a stable canonical URL, a cleaned or factual meta description, Open Graph and
Twitter Card metadata, and schema.org VideoObject JSON-LD built from cached
YouTube details. Catalogue pagination and each public ranking tab use
self-referencing canonical URLs. Empty, disabled, unavailable and personal
history pages are marked noindex. The new SEO configuration tab controls
catalogue and ranking indexation, structured data, social metadata and an
optional generic fallback description. Clean URLs, AggregateRating and video
sitemaps remain outside this release.

Version 0.16.1 makes robots directives unambiguous after removal of a
theme-level robots tag. Indexable public pages allow full text, image and video
previews. Personal history and unavailable pages output only noindex,follow;
Videos administration pages output only noindex,nofollow. Private pages no
longer receive canonical, Open Graph or Twitter metadata.

This version also adds three separately configurable visible FAQ areas. The
first non-empty catalogue page explains selection, updates, ratings and
privacy. Each ranking tab explains its own local calculation. Valid watch
pages expose factual information derived from cached video and rating data.
The accessible details/summary markup works without JavaScript. Optional
FAQPage JSON-LD exactly mirrors visible answers, is never added to noindex
pages and remains disabled by default.

Version 0.16.2 completes both Geeklog 2.1.1 administration integrations. The
Videos entry is exposed through plugin_getadminoption_videos in the permanent
administration menu and through plugin_cclabel_videos in the Plugins section
of the administration home page. Both entries require videos.admin and name
the Videos Admin group. A bundled SVG replaces the previously missing PNG.

Configuration initialization is now idempotent. Installation and the
0.16.1-to-0.16.2 update inspect every expected subgroup, tab, fieldset and
parameter. Missing entries are created with the current installation default;
existing administrator values are never overwritten. At runtime, defaults are
also merged beneath stored values so a missing optional entry cannot break a
request before the repair has run.

Version 0.16.3 lets a visitor delete one active rating without deleting the
corresponding viewing history. Registered accounts can do so directly from My
Videos with Geeklog CSRF protection. Registered and anonymous visitors can
also remove their current rating from a watch page after the pseudonymous
playback token has been established. The operation is rate-limited and
idempotent. It updates the segmented rating file and, for accounts, the
personal rating index, then rebuilds rating statistics, video and channel
rankings and marks the permanent pool for recalculation.

Version 0.16.4 fixes fresh automated installation after the idempotent
configuration work. Geeklog loads install_defaults.php from inside
plugin_load_configuration_videos, so a plain assignment created
$_VID_DEFAULT in the caller's local scope. Defaults are now assigned explicitly
through $GLOBALS and validated before use. Fresh installation also has a
restricted recovery mode: while the plugin is not yet registered, existing
null or empty values left by a failed installation are restored only when the
expected default is non-empty. Normal upgrades never replace an existing
administrator value.

Version 0.16.5 removes the remaining dependency on the lifetime of the
temporary $_VID_DEFAULT global during Geeklog autoinstallation. An immutable
JSON copy of the defaults is created when install_defaults.php first runs and
videos_default_configuration() can reconstruct the array after Geeklog
reinitializes configuration. Both operands are validated before array_merge,
so a missing transient global can no longer abort a fresh installation.

Version 0.17.0 adds a shared discovery reservoir designed for a catalogue of
several hundred videos. Compact references are distributed over ten bounded
JSON files while full records continue to use the existing per-video cache.
The default capacity is 500 references and the public catalogue exposes up to
300 of them through pagination. A manual seed can run eight distinct searches
of 50 results; duplicates, blocked content, unavailable videos and the active
Shorts policy are filtered. This initial operation costs about 800 YouTube
quota units and is therefore explicit in administration.

Renewal is lazy and shared: at most one search is attempted every 24 hours by
default, never one search per visitor. Part of the discovery queries uses
order=date plus publishedAfter, while the remainder uses relevance, views and
rating with controlled keyword subsets. The displayed reservoir targets 30%
videos published during the last 12 months and rotates deterministically each
UTC day. If quota or YouTube is unavailable, the existing reservoir remains
usable. Administrators can configure capacity, catalogue size, seed search
count, refresh interval, recent-video percentage and recent age window.

Each record stores a bounded internal reason, UTC date and keyed hash of the
moderating Geeklog account. It stores no administrator name, email or clear
user ID. Technical logging remains limited and contains only entity IDs.
